Don't block the event loop while waiting for a browser peer.

Both signalling error handlers wait for the browser with time.sleep(2). They
run on the asyncio loop that also hosts the signalling server, so every retry
froze the whole server for two seconds. The audio signaller retries for the
whole rental (Gleem's page never registers audio peer 3), so the server was
stalled nearly all the time: greetings took ~4 s to register and peer
cleanup ~5 s, and a browser that reconnected inside that window was rejected
as a duplicate peer and left on "Negotiating the video stream…"
(gleem-gg/machine-agent#18).

Applied at image build time against the installed package; see
vendor/selkies/README.md. MPL-2.0, like the file it changes.

--- a/selkies_gstreamer/__main__.py
+++ b/selkies_gstreamer/__main__.py
@@ -537,7 +537,7 @@
     async def on_signalling_error(e):
        if isinstance(e, WebRTCSignallingErrorNoPeer):
            # Waiting for peer to connect, retry in 2 seconds.
-           time.sleep(2)
+           await asyncio.sleep(2)
            await signalling.setup_call()
        else:
            logger.error("signalling error: %s", str(e))
@@ -545,7 +545,7 @@
     async def on_audio_signalling_error(e):
        if isinstance(e, WebRTCSignallingErrorNoPeer):
            # Waiting for peer to connect, retry in 2 seconds.
-           time.sleep(2)
+           await asyncio.sleep(2)
            await audio_signalling.setup_call()
        else:
            logger.error("signalling error: %s", str(e))
